{"task_id": "Mbpp/70", "solution": "def get_equal(tuples: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if all given tuples in a list have the same length.\n    Returns True if all tuples have equal length, or if the list is empty/contains a single tuple.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not tuples:\n        return True  # An empty list is considered to have all tuples of equal length (vacuously true).\n\n    if len(tuples) == 1:\n        return True  # A list with one tuple is considered to have all tuples of equal length.\n\n    # Get the length of the first tuple to use as a reference\n    reference_length = len(tuples[0])\n\n    # Iterate through the rest of the tuples and compare their lengths\n    for i in range(1, len(tuples)):\n        if len(tuples[i]) != reference_length:\n            return False  # Found a tuple with a different length\n\n    return True  # All tuples have the same length"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/74", "solution": "def is_samepatterns(array1: list, array2: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"\n    Checks if two arrays follow the same structural pattern.\n    Compares element relationships and consistency.\n    \"\"\"\n    if len(array1) != len(array2):\n        return False\n\n    # Handle the case where both arrays are empty\n    if not array1 and not array2:\n        return True\n    \n    # Create a mapping for each array\n    # This maps each unique element to the *first* index it appeared at.\n    # If the pattern is \"a\", \"b\", \"b\", the map would be {\"a\": 0, \"b\": 1}\n    # Then we compare the sequence of these mapped indices.\n    \n    map1 = {}\n    pattern1 = []\n    for i, item in enumerate(array1):\n        if item not in map1:\n            map1[item] = i\n        pattern1.append(map1[item])\n\n    map2 = {}\n    pattern2 = []\n    for i, item in enumerate(array2):\n        if item not in map2:\n            map2[item] = i\n        pattern2.append(map2[item])\n\n    return pattern1 == pattern2"}
