{"task_id": "Mbpp/301", "solution": "def dict_depth(dictionary: dict) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the maximum nesting depth of dictionaries within the given dictionary.\n    An empty dictionary or a dictionary with only non-dictionary values has a depth of 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    def _calculate_depth(obj, current_depth):\n        \"\"\"\n        Recursive helper function to traverse dictionary structures and find the maximum depth.\n        \"\"\"\n        max_nested_depth = current_depth\n        if isinstance(obj, dict):\n            for value in obj.values():\n                max_nested_depth = max(max_nested_depth, _calculate_depth(value, current_depth + 1))\n        return max_nested_depth\n\n    return _calculate_depth(dictionary, 1)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/305", "solution": "def start_withp(input_list: list[str]) -> tuple[str, str]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Return exactly two words starting with letter 'p' from input list.\n    \"\"\"\n    p_words = []\n    for s in input_list:\n        words = s.split()\n        for word in words:\n            if word.startswith('p'):\n                p_words.append(word)\n                if len(p_words) == 2:\n                    return (p_words[0], p_words[1])\n    return (p_words[0], p_words[1]) # This line assumes there will always be at least two words. If not, it would raise an IndexError. Based on the design, \"Undefined behavior if fewer than two 'p' words exist\" means we don't need to handle that specific edge case with explicit error handling."}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/300", "solution": "import math\n\ndef count_binary_seq(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Counts binary sequences of length 2n where the sum of the first n bits equals the sum of the last n bits.\n    This count is equivalent to the central binomial coefficient C(2n, n).\n    \"\"\"\n    # The number of ways to choose k bits to be 1 in the first half (n bits) is C(n, k).\n    # The number of ways to choose k bits to be 1 in the second half (n bits) is C(n, k).\n    # The total count is the sum of (C(n, k) * C(n, k)) for k from 0 to n, which simplifies to C(2n, n).\n    result = math.comb(2 * n, n)\n    return float(result)"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/306", "solution": "def max_sum_increasing_subseq(arr, length, i, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence from the prefix of the array\n    (up to index i) and also including the element at index k (where k > i).\n    \"\"\"\n    # dp[x] stores the maximum sum of an increasing subsequence ending at index x\n    # considering elements from arr[0] to arr[i].\n    dp = [0] * (i + 1)\n\n    # Calculate dp values for the prefix up to index i\n    for j in range(i + 1):\n        dp[j] = arr[j] # Base case: subsequence containing only arr[j]\n\n        # Iterate over previous elements to find potential increasing subsequences\n        for prev_j in range(j):\n            if arr[prev_j] < arr[j]:\n                dp[j] = max(dp[j], dp[prev_j] + arr[j])\n\n    # The element at index k must be included in the final subsequence.\n    k_element = arr[k]\n\n    # Find the maximum sum from the dp array (for elements up to index i)\n    # such that the last element arr[j] is less than arr[k].\n    # Initialize with 0 to allow for an empty prefix subsequence if no suitable elements are found.\n    prefix_max_sum = 0 \n\n    for j in range(i + 1):\n        if arr[j] < k_element:\n            prefix_max_sum = max(prefix_max_sum, dp[j])\n\n    # The final sum is the maximum valid prefix sum plus the element at index k.\n    return prefix_max_sum + k_element"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/308", "solution": "import heapq\n\ndef large_product(list1, list2, count):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the specified number of largest products from two given lists.\n    Selects one factor from each list to form a product.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not list1 or not list2 or count <= 0:\n        return []\n\n    # Sort both lists in descending order for positive numbers and ascending for negative numbers\n    # This allows us to easily find the largest and smallest magnitudes for product combinations.\n    sorted_list1 = sorted(list1)\n    sorted_list2 = sorted(list2)\n\n    # Use a min-heap to keep track of the 'count' largest products found so far.\n    # The smallest element in the heap is popped if a larger product is found.\n    min_heap = []\n\n    # Strategy:\n    # The largest products will come from combinations of:\n    # 1. Largest positive numbers * largest positive numbers\n    # 2. Smallest negative numbers * smallest negative numbers (positive product)\n    # 3. Largest positive number * smallest negative number (potentially large negative product, but we are looking for largest overall)\n\n    # Consider products from the 'ends' of the sorted lists.\n    # Iterate through potential candidates for large products.\n    # Since we need `count` largest products, and the lists can be long,\n    # we don't want to generate all n*m products if n*m is very large.\n    # However, the design context specifies O(n*m*log(n*m)) complexity,\n    # implying generation of all products followed by sorting/selection.\n\n    # Generate all possible products\n    all_products = []\n    for num1 in list1:\n        for num2 in list2:\n            all_products.append(num1 * num2)\n\n    # Sort all products in descending order\n    all_products.sort(reverse=True)\n\n    # Return the top 'count' products\n    return all_products[:count]"}
