{"task_id": "Mbpp/389", "solution": "def find_lucas(n):\n    \"\"\"\n    Calculates the n'th Lucas number.\n    Handles n=0, n=1 as base cases and iteratively calculates for n > 1.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"Input must be a non-negative integer\")\n    if n == 0:\n        return 2\n    if n == 1:\n        return 1\n\n    a, b = 2, 1  # L(0), L(1)\n    for _ in range(2, n + 1):\n        a, b = b, a + b\n    return b"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/388", "solution": "def highest_Power_of_2(n: int) -> int:\n    \"\"\"\n    Find the highest power of 2 that is less than or equal to n.\n    Uses bit manipulation for O(log n) performance.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n < 1:\n        # Per design context, assume positive integer inputs.\n        # However, for robustness, handling n=0 or negative n would typically return an error or specific value.\n        # For this problem, n must be positive as powers of 2 are positive.\n        return 0 # Or raise ValueError(\"Input must be a positive integer\")\n\n    # If n is already a power of 2, this method works correctly.\n    # Otherwise, it finds the next higher power of 2 and then halves it.\n    \n    # Decrement n by 1 to handle cases where n is a power of 2.\n    # E.g., if n=8 (1000), n-1=7 (0111).\n    # This ensures that the subsequent bit shifts find the bits just below n.\n    n -= 1 \n    \n    # Fill all bits to the right of the most significant bit with 1s.\n    # E.g., if n was originally 10 (1010), n-1 is 9 (1001).\n    # n |= n >> 1 becomes 1101 (1001 | 0100)\n    # n |= n >> 2 becomes 1111 (1101 | 0011)\n    # n |= n >> 4 becomes 1111 (1111 | 0000)\n    # For n=10 (binary 1010), n-1 is 9 (binary 1001).\n    # 1001 | 0100 = 1101\n    # 1101 | 0011 = 1111\n    # 1111 | 0000 = 1111\n    n |= n >> 1\n    n |= n >> 2\n    n |= n >> 4\n    n |= n >> 8\n    n |= n >> 16 # Covers up to 2^32-1\n    n |= n >> 32 # Covers up to 2^64-1\n\n    # After these operations, n will be of the form 00..011..1 (all 1s from the MSB down).\n    # Adding 1 to this number will give the smallest power of 2 greater than the original n.\n    # E.g., 1111 + 1 = 10000 (which is 16).\n    # Then we divide by 2 to get the highest power of 2 less than or equal to the original n.\n    # 16 / 2 = 8.\n    return (n + 1) >> 1 # Equivalent to (n + 1) // 2"}
{"task_id": "Mbpp/390", "solution": "def add_string(input_list, format_string):\n    \"\"\"\n    Applies a given format string to all elements in a list, returning a new list of formatted strings.\n    Raises ValueError if the format_string does not contain the '{0}' placeholder.\n    \"\"\"\n    if '{0}' not in format_string:\n        raise ValueError(\"Format string must contain '{0}' placeholder for element insertion.\")\n\n    formatted_elements = []\n    for element in input_list:\n        # Convert element to string before formatting as per design, though .format() often handles this.\n        element_str = str(element)\n        formatted_elements.append(format_string.format(element_str))\n    \n    return formatted_elements"}
