{"task_id": "Mbpp/597", "solution": "def find_kth(arr1, arr2, k):\n    \"\"\"\n    Finds the kth smallest element from two sorted arrays using a binary search approach.\n    Assumes k is 1-indexed and within the valid range of combined array lengths.\n    \"\"\"\n    m, n = len(arr1), len(arr2)\n\n    # Ensure arr1 is the shorter array for optimized binary search\n    if m > n:\n        arr1, arr2 = arr2, arr1\n        m, n = n, m\n\n    # Define the search range for the partition in arr1 (cut1)\n    # cut1 must be at least max(0, k - n) to ensure cut2 (k - cut1) is not greater than n\n    # cut1 must be at most min(m, k) to ensure cut1 is not greater than m or k\n    low = max(0, k - n)\n    high = min(m, k)\n\n    while low <= high:\n        cut1 = (low + high) // 2\n        cut2 = k - cut1\n\n        # Determine the four elements around the cuts\n        # L1: element before cut1 in arr1, R1: element at cut1 in arr1\n        # L2: element before cut2 in arr2, R2: element at cut2 in arr2\n        L1 = float('-inf') if cut1 == 0 else arr1[cut1 - 1]\n        R1 = float('inf') if cut1 == m else arr1[cut1]\n\n        L2 = float('-inf') if cut2 == 0 else arr2[cut2 - 1]\n        R2 = float('inf') if cut2 == n else arr2[cut2]\n\n        # Check if the partition is valid\n        if L1 <= R2 and L2 <= R1:\n            # Found the correct partition\n            return max(L1, L2)\n        elif L1 > R2:\n            # cut1 is too large, move to the left side of arr1\n            high = cut1 - 1\n        else: # L2 > R1\n            # cut1 is too small, move to the right side of arr1\n            low = cut1 + 1\n\n    # This line should ideally not be reached if k is always valid (1 <= k <= m+n)\n    return -1 # Fallback, as per problem context avoiding exceptions."}
