{"task_id": "Mbpp/603", "solution": "def get_ludic(limit: int) -> list[int]:\n    \"\"\"\n    Generates all ludic numbers up to a given limit using a sieve-like process.\n    It iteratively removes elements from a working list based on the last found ludic number.\n    \"\"\"\n    if limit < 1:\n        return []\n\n    # Initialize a list of candidate numbers from 1 up to the limit.\n    working_list = list(range(1, limit + 1))\n    ludic_numbers = []\n\n    # Continue the sieve process as long as there are numbers in the working list.\n    while len(working_list) > 0:\n        # The first number in the working list is the next ludic number.\n        current_ludic = working_list[0]\n        ludic_numbers.append(current_ludic)\n        working_list.pop(0)  # Remove the current ludic number from candidates.\n\n        # If the current ludic number is 1, it doesn't sieve any numbers itself\n        # from the remaining sequence (as per definition or implicit behaviour).\n        if current_ludic == 1:\n            continue\n\n        # Collect indices of numbers to remove from the current working list.\n        # These are elements whose 1-based position in the *current* working_list\n        # is a multiple of current_ludic.\n        indices_to_remove = []\n        for i in range(len(working_list) // current_ludic):\n            # Calculate the 0-based index: (i+1) * current_ludic - 1\n            index = (i + 1) * current_ludic - 1\n            if index < len(working_list):\n                indices_to_remove.append(index)\n            else:\n                break # No more elements at this multiple\n\n        # Remove elements in reverse order of index to prevent index shifting issues.\n        for index in reversed(indices_to_remove):\n            working_list.pop(index)\n\n    return ludic_numbers"}
